/* scmrt.c -- S-MARV runtime: Skint-style runtime support for code
 * generated by the S-MARV compiler (see smarv.html SS9 and the
 * interface in scmrt.mvh).
 *
 * This file is written in the WCPL C dialect, in the same
 * single-source style as the other adapter libraries: compiled with
 * wcpl -c it becomes the $scmrt module of a WCPL binary; compiled
 * with a plain C compiler the exported names are prefixed scmrt_
 * (via the #define block below), matching what the MARV C target
 * expects ($scmrt:collect -> scmrt_collect, and so on).  The only
 * other #ifdefs are for WCPL intrinsics (see mk_flonum/flo_val).
 *
 * What lives here (everything else -- tagging, bump allocation,
 * block access, procedure calls -- is inlined by the compiler):
 *   - heap setup and a downward Cheney copying collector,
 *   - symbol interning and the 991-bucket global store,
 *   - Skint-format literal deserialization (rds_sexp),
 *   - rest-argument list building, cold error paths,
 *   - eqv? / equal?.
 *
 * Memory layout.  One power-of-two-sized region is divided as
 *      [ static area (2S words) ][ semispace 0 (S) ][ semispace 1 (S) ]
 * where S = the (pow2-rounded) heap size in words.  $hmask is built
 * over the whole region, so the compiler's inlined notobjptr test
 * accepts pointers into the static area and both semispaces alike.
 * Literals (deserialize_sexp) and global store boxes (global_loc)
 * are allocated in the static area: they are immortal and never
 * move, which is why those entry points legitimately ignore their
 * $hp argument.  The static area is scanned as a root set by the
 * collector, so heap objects referenced from literals and global
 * boxes stay live and the cells are updated in place on a flip.
 * The active semispace base is exported as $heap_limit and updated
 * by collect; allocation bumps $hp downward toward it.
 *
 * Heap growth (Skint's cxm_hgc "bump", k.c): when, after a collection,
 * the live objects plus the requested words -- with Skint's
 * make-heap-half-empty margin of twice the live size -- do not fit in
 * a semispace, the semispaces are doubled (repeatedly) and the live
 * objects are relocated into the enlarged region, which replaces the
 * old one (the static area is copied across and its pointers rebased).
 * If the enlarged region cannot be allocated, the collector prints an
 * error message and exits with exit code 1.
 *
 * $r (the root/scratch register array) is owned by the caller
 * (compiled code keeps it in a module data segment); the reg_words
 * argument of init is used as the initial capacity of the
 * registered-root table.  init returns the initial $hp (the top of
 * the active semispace).
 *
 * Forwarding: a copied object's old header word is replaced by
 * (new_obj | 2).  Block headers always have bit 0 set, and Native
 * (cxtype) headers are aligned pointers with bit 1 clear, so bit 1
 * alone marks a forwarding pointer during collection.
 */

#if defined(__WCPL__)
#pragma module "scmrt"
#endif

